<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The table shows the milk sold, in litres (to the nearest litre), on <m>{73}</m> days at a dairy kiosk in Nakuru.</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Milk (litres)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m>–<m>{19}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m>–<m>{29}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m>–<m>{39}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m>–<m>{49}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{50}</m>–<m>{59}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{60}</m>–<m>{69}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{3}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{11}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{16}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{13}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>For grouped data, a quartile, decile or percentile at a given position is estimated by <me> L + \frac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c </me>where <m>L</m> is the lower class boundary of the class that holds the position, <m>CF</m> the cumulative frequency before that class, <m>F</m> its frequency and <m>c</m> the class width.</p><p>Give each answer correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p>(a) Find the lower quartile, <m>Q_1</m>.</p><div><m>Q_1 =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/> litres  </div><p>(b) Find the upper quartile, <m>Q_3</m>.</p><div><m>Q_3 =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> litres  </div><p>(c) Find the interquartile range.</p><div>IQR <m>=</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/> litres  </div><p>(d) Find the quartile deviation.</p><div>QD <m>=</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans4"/> litres  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>For grouped data, <m>Q_1</m> is at position <m>n/4</m> and <m>Q_3</m> at <m>3n/4</m>. The amounts are to the nearest litre, so each class starts at its lower class boundary (the class <m>{10}</m>–<m>{19}</m> starts at <m>{9.5}</m>).</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Class</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Cumulative frequency (cf)</cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m>–<m>{19}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{3}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{3}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m>–<m>{29}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{13}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m>–<m>{39}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{11}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{24}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m>–<m>{49}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{16}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{50}</m>–<m>{59}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{60}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{60}</m>–<m>{69}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{13}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{73}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>(a) Position <m>{73} \div 4 = {18.25}</m>, in the class <m>{30}</m>–<m>{39}</m>: <me>Q_1 = {29.5} + \frac{ {18.25} - {13} }{ {11} } \times 10 \approx {34.3}</me></p><p>(b) Position <m>3 \times {73} \div 4 = {54.75}</m>, in the class <m>{50}</m>–<m>{59}</m>: <me>Q_3 = {49.5} + \frac{ {54.75} - {40} }{ {20} } \times 10 \approx {56.9}</me></p><p>(c) The IQR is the spread of the middle half of the data. Using the unrounded quartiles, <m>Q_3 - Q_1 \approx {22.60}</m>, so IQR <m>\approx {22.6}</m> litres. Subtracting the rounded quartiles gives the same value to 1 decimal place.</p><p>(d) QD <m>= (Q_3 - Q_1)/2 \approx {11.3}</m> litres. Half of a rounded IQR, rounded again to 1 decimal place, is also accepted.</p><p>Note: if you used the class limits instead of the boundaries for both quartiles, both are half a litre too large, so the IQR comes out right. The quartiles themselves are still wrong.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{377}{11}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{455}{8}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{1989}{88}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{1989}{176}</m></p></answer>
